<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julio César Méndez Montenegro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julio César Méndez <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> (1915–1996) was a Guatemalan lawyer and politician who served as president from 1966 to 1970, the only civilian to hold the office between 1954 and 1986. Elected for the Revolutionary Party, he accepted army control over security in return for taking office, and during his term the army ran a brutal counter-insurgency campaign in the east of the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julio-cesar-mendez-montenegro.jpg" alt="Julio César Méndez Montenegro" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobierno de Guatemala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foto_oficial_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_M%C3%A9ndez_Montenegro_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Julio César Méndez Montenegro was born on 23 November 1915 and made his career as a law professor. In 1966 he was elected on a platform promising democratic reforms and limits on military power, and his election and swearing-in were seen as a turning point after long military rule. At the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo">Juan José Arévalo</a>, president from 1945 to 1951, was the only civilian to have completed a full presidential term since independence. The death squad Mano Blanca, set up in 1966 as a front for the MLN, was founded with the specific aim of preventing him from taking power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Within days of Méndez taking office, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> sent Colonel John Webber Jr. to modernise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s counter-insurgency forces, expanding training of the 5,000-man army and setting up counter-terror units under US police advisers. In exchange for the army's support of his government, Méndez authorised it to use any means necessary against the insurgency. Under Colonel Carlos Manuel Arana Osorio, called the Butcher of Zacapa, the army waged its first major anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> offensive, most intense in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Zacapa, with massacres of civilians and the destruction of peasant communities.</p><p>Observers estimate that as many as 15,000 Guatemalans were killed by the military and government death squads in three years to eliminate fewer than 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>; Amnesty International cited 3,000 to 8,000 peasants killed. In March 1966 thirty associates of the Guatemalan Party of Labour were kidnapped, tortured and killed, one of the first major forced disappearances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. From January 1967 the Special Commando Unit of the Guatemalan Army (SCUGA) under Colonel Máximo Zepeda ran most urban counter-insurgency, with abductions, bombings, torture and executions, often under the guise of fictitious death squads.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> and killed the American ambassador John Gordon Mein in 1968 and the West German ambassador Karl von Spreti in 1970. Mano Blanca killed Méndez's first cousin César Montenegro Paniagua in retribution for von Spreti's death. Méndez left office in July 1970 and died on 30 April 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julio César Méndez Montenegro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Guatemala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2075 1619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julio César Méndez Montenegro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2525 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2525 1889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
